# Cache rules for the Cloudflare Workers deployment.
#
# Everything here is a static file published by editing it in place — the URL
# never changes when the contents do. Left to the defaults, a member who has
# opened the app once keeps running the copy their browser and Cloudflare's
# edge already hold: a new schedule is published, the leaders see it, and the
# members' phones carry on showing last week's until something evicts it.
#
# `no-cache` does NOT mean "don't cache". It means "cache it, but revalidate
# before using it". The browser still keeps the file and still sends
# If-None-Match; when nothing has changed the answer is a 304 with no body, so
# this costs a round trip rather than a re-download. That is the right trade
# for files that are the whole point of the app being up to date.
#
# The apps also cache-bust their own data reads with ?t=, which handles a page
# left open. This handles the other half: the page itself.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/*.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The pretty per-church links (/c/shekinah/) and the site root both serve
# index.html without naming it, so the glob above doesn't reach them.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/c/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Fonts, icons and images are content-addressed by name and rarely change;
# letting them cache normally keeps the app fast on a phone.
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The text of Scripture is the one exception to the JSON rule above. A book
# file only changes if somebody re-runs scripts/build-next-bible.mjs and
# commits the result, and there are 198 of them totalling 14 MB — revalidating
# each one on every read costs a phone in Kuwait a round trip for an answer
# that is always "unchanged".
#
# This rule sits last on purpose. Cloudflare applies the rules that match, and
# where two set the same header the later one wins; if that ever changed, the
# fallback is the /*.json rule above, which is what happens today. So this can
# only help.
#
# Note this is the browser's cache, not the app's: flcc-next/sw.js already
# keeps Scripture cache-first and never refetches it, so a corrected Bible
# reaches a device when the service worker's VERSION is bumped, whatever any
# header here says. The build script prints that reminder.
/flcc-next/bible/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
